@style(../styles/IDocDemo.style)
@title(Octo 16: symbolic maths)

`syms` declares symbols; expressions built from them stay exact until you ask for numbers.

@section(Expressions)

`expand`, `factor`, `simplify` and `subs` rewrite and evaluate an expression.

@octo
syms x y
e = (x + 1)^2
expand(e)
factor(x^2 - 1)
simplify(sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2)
subs(e, x, 2)
vpa(sqrt(sym(2)), 20)
@end

@section(Calculus)

`diff`, `int`, `limit` and `taylor` work as on paper.

@octo
syms x
diff(x^3 + sin(x), x)
int(x^2, x)
int(x^2, x, 0, 3)
limit(sin(x) / x, x, 0)
taylor(exp(x), x, 0, "Order", 4)
@end

@section(Solving)

`solve` finds exact roots and solves systems; `dsolve` solves differential equations.

@octo
syms x y
solve(x^2 - 5*x + 6 == 0, x)
[a, b] = solve(x + y == 3, x - y == 1, x, y)
syms f(t)
dsolve(diff(f, t) == -2*f, f(0) == 1)
@end

@section(Back to numbers)

`double` gives a number, and `subs` inside a handle turns an expression into a function you can call.

@octo
syms x
g = x^2 - 2;
r = solve(g == 0, x); double(r)'
h = @(t) double(subs(g, x, t)); h(3)
@end
